lities. Periodically, we evaluate our income tax positions based on current tax law and positions taken by various tax auditors within the jurisdictions where we are required to file income tax returns.\n\nIncome tax expense was $3.4 million and $2.2 million for the three months ended March 31, 2026 and 2025, respectively. The effective tax rate for the three months ended March 31, 2026 and 2025 was 27.5% and 29.2%, respectively. The decrease compared to the first quarter of 2025 was primarily due to discrete tax benefits related to the vesting of equity awards."}],"net":[{"heading":null,"text":"Net interest income is the most significant contributor to our net income and is the difference between the interest and fees earned on interest-earning assets and the interest expense incurred in connection with interest-bearing liabilities. Net interest income is primarily a function of the average balances and yields of these interest-earning assets and interest-bearing liabilities. These factors are influenced by internal considerations such as product mix and risk appetite as well as external influences such as economic conditions, competition for loans and deposits and market interest rates."}]},"capex_src":{"concept":"us-gaap:PaymentsToAcquirePropertyPlantAndEquipment","label":"Purchase of premises and equipment","how":"xbrl"},"src":{"revenue":{"c":"us-gaap:RevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax","l":"Revenue from Contract with Customer, Excluding Assessed Tax","ytd":false,"how":"xbrl"},"sm":{"c":"us-gaap:MarketingAndAdvertisingExpense","l":"Advertising and marketing","ytd":false,"how":"xbrl"},"pretax":{"c":"us-gaap:IncomeLossFromContinuingOperationsBeforeIncomeTaxesExtraordinaryItemsNoncontrollingInterest","l":"us-gaap_Income Loss From Continuing Operations Before Income Taxes Extraordinary Items Noncontrolling Interest","ytd":false,"how":"xbrl"},"tax":{"c":"us-gaap:IncomeTaxExpenseBenefit","l":"Provision for income taxes","ytd":false,"how":"xbrl"},"ni":